For the software side of this project, my main goal was getting a working, 
responsive flight control and tuning loop running cleanly without getting into rtos. 
I structured the code around small classes (`Websocket`, `MPU6050`, `PIDController`, and `Motor`) 
so that each part of the system has a clear responsibilityand can be debugged on its own.

Instead of relying on a physical RC transmitter, I hosted a local Wi-Fi access point 
(`Mini_Drone_AP`) on the ESP32 and served a browser based joystick UI over WebSocket (`/ws`).
This let me control throttle, yaw, pitch, and roll from a phone or laptop, test PID adjustments live, and watch roll behavior on a chart.

On the control side, I read accelerometer and gyroscope data from an MPU6050 over I2C,
fuse the angle estimate with a complementary filter, and run separate PID controllers for pitch, roll, and yaw 
with integral clamping and output limits. Because early flight and bench tests inevitably bring disconnects or dropped packets, 
I added basic fail-safe checks—such as zeroing throttle on disconnect or after a `500 ms` packet timeout.

## Hardware & Pinout

| Component | ESP32 Pin / Config | Notes |
| **MPU6050 SDA** | `GPIO 21` | I2C data |
| **MPU6050 SCL** | `GPIO 19` | I2C clock |
| **Top-Left Motor (`TLMotor`)** | `GPIO 32` | LEDC Channel `1`, 24 kHz, 8-bit PWM |
| **Top-Right Motor (`TRMotor`)** | `GPIO 27` | LEDC Channel `0`, 24 kHz, 8-bit PWM |
| **Bottom-Left Motor (`BLMotor`)** | `GPIO 25` | LEDC Channel `2`, 24 kHz, 8-bit PWM |
| **Bottom-Right Motor (`BRMotor`)** | `GPIO 12` | LEDC Channel `3`, 24 kHz, 8-bit PWM *(strapping pin — check boot behavior)* |

> **Safety Note:** Always remove propellers or secure the frame safely before testing motor spin directions and PID responses.

